{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjYCGAjvGHjJmm2EbEBr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HWKvoVkiFPG8cFZQhGLxRX","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with a clef and a key signature of two sharps. The first stretch is two beamed groups of four 16ths on low D. That is eight 16th D2s, a written-out form of the edition's \"D2 half tremolo, 16ths\", so it is the first half of bar 54. Next comes a low 8th, D2. Then the clef changes to a higher clef with the sign that looks like a \"B\". After it is a slurred D4 16th, C#4 16th and E4 8th, then a low A2 8th after the slur. That finishes bar 54: half-note tremolo + D2 8th + (D4 C#4 E4) + A2. A barline follows. Next is D3 8th, (D4 16th, C#4 16th, E4 8th), A2 8th. That figure appears twice, which matches bar 55 exactly. The line ends at a barline after bar 55. Bar 56's alternating D3 and A2 8ths do not appear, so the line holds bars 54–55. The only difference from the edition is notation: the copyist writes the tremolo out as 16ths and uses a clef change where the edition does not.","right":true,"insights":"Bar 55 was easy: its figure repeats twice and the barlines are clear. The opening was the hard part. The copyist writes the tremolo out as eight 16ths and changes clef mid-bar, so the first half of bar 54 looks just like bars 51–53. Only the D2 / D4-C#4-E4 / A2 figure that follows shows it is bar 54. A slightly wider crop showing the previous line's end would have made the start certain."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5149,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5149},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":